.\" The manual for bin/git-cleanup, which is what `git cleanup --help` opens.
.\" Git turns --help on any `git <command>` into `man git-<command>` before
.\" the command runs, an external one included, so without this page the
.\" answer is "No manual entry for git-cleanup". Arguments after a subcommand
.\" are not touched by git, so `git cleanup worktrees --help` is the
.\" subcommand's own page, git-cleanup-worktrees.1.
.\"
.\" This page holds what every subcommand shares, so that the next one does
.\" not copy it: the flags, the file the editor opens, the environment and the
.\" exit status. The code behind it is lib/cleanup-lib.sh.
.TH GIT\-CLEANUP 1 "" "dotfiles" "dotfiles"
.SH NAME
git\-cleanup \- clear out what has gone stale in a git repository
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup
.I thing
[\fB\-n\fR | \fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists what a repository has collected, sorted by how safe each is to remove,
opens the list in your git editor, and does what the saved list says. Nothing is
removed that has work in it unless the line says so in as many words.
.PP
It is
.B git cleanup
rather than
.B git clean
because that is a git built\-in, and rather than a subcommand of
.B git worktree
because git does not let an external command extend a built\-in.
.SH THINGS
.TP
.BR worktrees ", " worktree ", " wt
Worktrees with no activity for 14 days. See
.BR git\-cleanup\-worktrees (1),
which \fBgit cleanup worktrees \-\-help\fR opens.
.TP
.BR branches ", " branch ", " br
Local branches that are merged or pushed and have had no activity for a week. See
.BR git\-cleanup\-branches (1),
which \fBgit cleanup branches \-\-help\fR opens.
.PP
The spellings match the aliases \fBgit wt\fR (for \fBworktree\fR) and \fBgit br\fR
(for \fBbranch\fR) in the git config.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the list as it would be written, with what would happen to each item by
default, and stop.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Clean the idle items now, without the editor and without a question. Only idle
ones: nothing that is locked, recent or has work is touched.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " N"
How long without activity counts as idle. The default is 14 for worktrees and 7
for branches.
.TP
.B \-h
The short help, with the words a line may start with. (\fB\-\-help\fR after a
thing opens that thing's manual page.)
.SH THE FILE
Every item is on its own line, in the first of five groups that fits it, and the
groups are listed in this order:
.IP "1 protected" 14
Never cleaned. Written as a comment with no word, so it cannot be turned on.
.IP "2 locked" 14
Kept by the user. Needs unlocking first.
.IP "3 has work" 14
Something would be lost: uncommitted changes, or commits nothing else holds.
.IP "4 recent" 14
Nothing to lose, but active within the last \fB\-\-days\fR days.
.IP "5 idle" 14
Nothing to lose and quiet for \fB\-\-days\fR days. The only group that starts
turned on.
.PP
The first word of a line says what happens to the item it names; each thing
documents its own words. A line starting with
.B #
and a space is off: take the
.B #
and the space off to turn it on, put them back or delete the line to skip it.
A line starting with
.B ##
is a note and is never read, which is why uncommenting the whole file
(\fB:%s/^# //\fR) turns every item on and leaves the notes and the protected
items alone.
.PP
Any other word, an unterminated reason in quotes, a reason on a word that does
not take one, an item that is not listed, one that is protected, or the same item
twice is an error: the editor opens again with the faults at the top of the file,
as \fBgit rebase \-i\fR does. Quit it with an error (\fB:cq\fR) to cancel.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
The default word never throws work away. Turning on a whole group is safe,
because git refuses to remove an item with work in it and so does this. Only the
stronger words, written on the line, do.
.IP \(bu 2
Each line is looked at again just before it is acted on. An item that has moved
to a riskier group while the editor was open (idle to recent, anything to has
work) is refused rather than changed on out\-of\-date information.
.IP \(bu 2
A failure or a refusal is reported and the rest carry on, with a non\-zero exit
at the end.
.IP \(bu 2
After the editor closes the plan is summarised and confirmed once more. Removal
cannot be undone the way a rebase can, and saving an unedited list applies its
defaults.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIT_EDITOR
The editor, as git picks it: \fBGIT_EDITOR\fR, then \fBcore.editor\fR, then
\fBVISUAL\fR and \fBEDITOR\fR.
.TP
.B CLEANUP_NOW
Seconds since the epoch, standing in for the clock. Only the tests set it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when everything asked for was done, or there was nothing to do. 1 when the
list was cancelled, or any item was refused or failed. 2 for a bad argument.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-cleanup\-worktrees (1),
.BR git\-cleanup\-branches (1),
.BR git\-worktree (1),
.BR git\-branch (1)
